  "body": "Greetings!\r\n\r\nReading the spec for select element, I saw that a \"multiple\" attribute exists since a while https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#the-select-element\r\n\r\nThis allows to select multiple elements in the list. But this assumes that the selection has no upper bound. The \"required\" attribute can define a lower bound of the selection (\"select at least 1 element\"), but if you want the selection to be limited to 2 or 3 elements, then you have no way to do so but relying on Javascript.\r\n\r\nWill it be considered as acceptable to build a standard attribute for upper bound of selected elements?\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nIn order to make this proposal more powerful, it could be considered to add the \"min\" and \"max\" attribute to \"select multiple\" tags, with the meaning of \"setting the min and max number of selectable elements\". Min would then be greater or equal to 2, with a default value of \"0\" (so it's backward compatible, and starting from \"2\" avoid issues with presence/absence of the \"required\" attribute). Max would be greater or equal to Min.\r\nThe same thing could be done with \"input checkbox\", allowing to set a range of inputs to check.",
